{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "NTiedtkeSaturationAdjustment",
    "description": "`cuadjtqn`, the moist-adjustment kernel of WRF's New Tiedtke scheme (`phys/physics_mmm/cu_ntiedtke.F90:3256-3402`, WRF 4.8). It is the single most reused routine in the scheme: the environmental saturation in `cuinin`, the test parcel in `cutypen`, the updraught condensation in `cuascn` and `cubasmcn`, and the downdraught evaporation in `cudlfsn` and `cuddrafn` all go through it, several hundred times per column per model step.\n\nWhat it does is one thing in three dresses. Given a pressure and an unadjusted (T, q), it takes TWO Newton steps towards saturation, each step being\n\n    dq = (q - q_sat(T,p)) / (1 + q_sat dq_sat/dT L/cp)\n    T <- T + (L/cp) dq,   q <- q - dq\n\nwith every thermodynamic quantity mixed-phase through the single liquid-fraction weight of `moist_thermodynamics.esm`. The three `kcall` modes differ only in which increments they admit:\n\n* `kcall = 0`, the ENVIRONMENT (`env`): both steps unconditional and unsigned. Used to put the environmental temperature and humidity on the saturation curve at half levels.\n* `kcall = 1`, the UPDRAUGHT (`up`): CONDENSATION only. The whole adjustment is skipped unless the first increment is positive, so a subsaturated rising parcel is left alone rather than dried.\n* `kcall = 2`, the DOWNDRAUGHT (`dd`): EVAPORATION only. The first increment is clipped at zero, so a saturated sinking parcel is left alone rather than condensed into.\n\nThey are exposed here as three separate pairs of observeds computed from the same input, rather than as one pair behind a mode switch, because that is what they are: three call sites wanting three different behaviours out of one piece of thermodynamics. A component that needs the updraught form reads `T_up`; nothing has to carry a mode code.\n\nTHE SAME NEWTON STEP IS WRITTEN TWO DIFFERENT WAYS IN THE ONE ROUTINE, and both spellings are kept here. The `kcall = 0` and `kcall = 2` branches form the saturation correction with `zcor = 1/(1 - eps q_sat)` folded into `q_sat` first; the `kcall = 1` branch keeps `zcor = 1 - eps q_sat` un-inverted and multiplies it through, giving `(q zcor^2 - q_sat zcor)/(zcor^2 + q_sat zf)`. Multiply the first by `zcor^2/zcor^2` and it becomes the second exactly -- they are one formula, and the scheme simply never rewrote one of them. The templates `newton_increment_folded` and `newton_increment_scaled` carry the two spellings and say so; they agree to round-off, and the .esm keeps both only so that neither branch is a re-derivation.\n\nSECOND-STEP GUARD. Both guarded modes discard the second Newton step when the first increment is below 1e-20 in magnitude -- `kcall = 1` sets it to zero outright, `kcall = 2` merely re-clips it at zero. The asymmetry is in the Fortran and is reproduced.\n\nThe component is POINTWISE, over an index set `pt` of independent adjustment states, because that is how the routine is used: each of its several hundred invocations per step is an independent (p, T, q) problem, and the reference is a tape of exactly those invocations.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "wrf",
      "convection",
      "new-tiedtke",
      "saturation-adjustment",
      "mixed-phase"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Tiedtke, M. (1989). A comprehensive mass flux scheme for cumulus parameterization in large-scale models. Mon. Wea. Rev. 117, 1779-1800.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1989)117<1779:ACMFSF>2.0.CO;2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR/MMM-physics cu_ntiedtke.F90 (WRF 4.8 phys/physics_mmm submodule), subroutine cuadjtqn."
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NPT": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 48,
      "description": "Number of independent adjustment states evaluated together. The routine is pointwise, so this axis carries whatever set of (p, T, q) states a consumer has: a column of half levels in `cuinin`, a column of updraught levels in `cuascn`, or -- in this component's own tests -- a fixed-size sample of the tape of one model step's calls. The default 48 is that sample size, and it is what a test gets, because an esm test cannot rebind a metaparameter: it is the smallest per-mode call count among the five reference steps (the downdraught mode at step 639), so one size serves every mode and every step."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "pt": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NPT"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "NTiedtkeSaturationAdjustment": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "./moist_thermodynamics.esm"
        }
      ],
      "subsystems": {
        "wrf": {
          "ref": "../../../lib/wrf_constants.esm"
        },
        "par": {
          "ref": "./ntiedtke_parameters.esm"
        }
      },
      "variables": {
        "p": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Pressure at which each state is adjusted. In the scheme this is a half-level pressure for the environment and the updraught and the same for the downdraught; the routine takes it as a bare argument."
        },
        "T_in": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Unadjusted temperature."
        },
        "q_in": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Unadjusted water-vapour mixing ratio."
        },
        "ldcpm_in": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "L/cp at the input temperature, the temperature rise per unit condensate. Named because each mode multiplies its first increment by it."
        },
        "c_env1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "First Newton increment of the environmental mode: unconditional and of either sign."
        },
        "T_env_mid": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Environmental temperature after the first Newton step."
        },
        "q_env_mid": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Environmental mixing ratio after the first Newton step."
        },
        "c_env2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Second environmental increment, evaluated at the mid state and likewise unguarded."
        },
        "T_env": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Environmental temperature after both Newton steps -- the `kcall = 0` result, which `cuinin` uses to put the half-level environment on its saturation curve."
        },
        "q_env": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Environmental mixing ratio after both Newton steps."
        },
        "c_up1_raw": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "First Newton increment of the updraught mode before the condensation test, in the scheme's un-inverted spelling."
        },
        "up_active": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "1 where the updraught mode does anything at all: the whole `kcall = 1` adjustment is inside `if (zcond > 0)`, so a subsaturated parcel passes through untouched."
        },
        "c_up1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "First updraught increment as applied: the raw value where condensation occurs, zero otherwise."
        },
        "T_up_mid": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught temperature after the first Newton step."
        },
        "q_up_mid": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught mixing ratio after the first Newton step."
        },
        "c_up2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Second updraught increment: zero where the mode is inactive, and also zero where the first increment was below 1e-20 -- the Fortran's `if (abs(zcond) < 1.e-20) zcond1 = 0.`, which refuses to refine a correction already at the resolution of the mixing ratio."
        },
        "T_up": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught temperature after the adjustment -- the `kcall = 1` result, the condensation-only form the rising parcel uses."
        },
        "q_up": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught mixing ratio after the adjustment."
        },
        "c_dd1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "First Newton increment of the downdraught mode, clipped at zero so that only evaporation is admitted."
        },
        "T_dd_mid": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught temperature after the first Newton step."
        },
        "q_dd_mid": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught mixing ratio after the first Newton step."
        },
        "c_dd2_raw": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Second downdraught increment before its conditional clip."
        },
        "c_dd2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Second downdraught increment as applied. It is clipped at zero ONLY where the first increment was below 1e-20; everywhere else it is admitted with either sign. That asymmetry against the updraught mode, which zeroes the second step instead, is the Fortran's and is not an oversight this transcription corrects."
        },
        "T_dd": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught temperature after the adjustment -- the `kcall = 2` result, the evaporation-only form the sinking parcel uses."
        },
        "q_dd": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "pt"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught mixing ratio after the adjustment."
        }
      },
