        {
          "id": "step2101_kcall1_up_adjustments",
          "description": "The UPDRAUGHT (kcall = 1) mode of cuadjtqn, on 48 of the calls WRF's own New Tiedtke made at one model step -- the condensation-only form `cutypen`, `cuascn` and `cubasmcn` use for a rising parcel, whose whole adjustment is skipped unless the first increment is positive. Reference run: the idealized single-column chem170 run of data/eqwefic/notes/chem170_scm_reference_run.md (em_scm_xy, Weisman-Klemp sounding over Kansas, 1997-06-20 12 UTC + 48 h, dx = 20 km, 59 levels, dt = 60 s, cu_physics = 16), step 2101: 17 LST on the second day, deep convection (ktype 1) with 5.5e-3 mm of rain. Reference build: the real64 (kind_phys = real64) replay of that step through kernels/ntiedtke_driver of WRF fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem170, with the seven constants cu_ntiedtke_init takes (cp, R_d, R_v, L_v, L_s, L_f, g) supplied at binary64 precision -- they are ARGUMENTS, so that is what a double-precision reference means, and it is what lib/wrf_constants.esm carries. The scheme's OWN parameter block is a separate matter and stays at its binary32 roundings (FORTRAN_BUGS N109). The values are a TAPE of every cuadjtqn invocation of the step, added to the diagnostic kernel for this component: nothing else publishes them, because they are in-place updates of arrays the caller owns and are overwritten many times before cumastrn returns. The tape records the input state, the state after the FIRST Newton step and the final state, so each half of the two-step adjustment is pinned separately rather than only their composition. The 48 calls are sampled evenly along the SORTED input temperature, so the ice branch, the mixed-phase ramp and the water branch of the phase weight are all exercised in every test rather than whichever the tape happened to start with. These are algebraic observeds at t = 0 and the document has no ODE state, so the empty time_span is a single static evaluation.",
